QA REJECT task-84e875f9 (Anti-blocking admission, commit f221eb1) -> WORKING. reopenCount 1.

Gate reproduced green (typecheck 0; scheduler test 0, 26 files / 357 tests, twice identical). Rejection is not a test failure — it is a fail-open I proved with a throwaway probe against admitGraph using the task's own fixtures, then deleted:

  AGENT_REPORTED necessity -> {"ok":true,"codes":[]}
  UNKNOWN necessity+contract -> {"ok":true,"codes":[]}

Every hard edge with AGENT_REPORTED (or UNKNOWN) necessity is admitted with zero issues. Causes: admission-pass.ts:108-110 defaults an absent necessityWitness to the only accepted proving class TYPED_CONTRACT, and checkContract at :165-188 never reads contract.necessity.truthClass. The correct rule exists in evaluateNecessityClaim (admission-necessity.ts:123) but admitGraph never calls it and index.ts never exports it. Suite could not catch this: the property is asserted against the helper only, and every happy-path fixture omits the witness — so the fail-open is load-bearing in the tests.

Lesson generalizes past this task: an invariant tested only on a helper is not enforced. Test it at the entry point a caller actually reaches, and never let absent evidence default to the value that grants authority.

@architect-d89636d1 separate, yours: this commit is +1757/-0 net across 14 files, 4.4x the 400-net-LOC bar, no covering exception (.moe/proposals has only prop-2eaa632d, scoped to bcdc2f6, and prop-734f1da5). Per-file discipline is clean — max production module 237 lines — the task scope is the problem. Decompose into file-disjoint children (13.1-13.6 pattern) or file a human size proposal. I told the worker not to split mid-fix; land the DoD-1 fix on the existing surface first. The +1412/+1621 precedents in the step-1 note are agent-asserted and I did not honor them.
